WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.160
AI-assisted coding can accelerate good decisions, but it can also accelerate dangerous ones.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.340
Security cannot be postponed until the end of the conversation.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:14.000
The same speed that helps a prototype appear quickly can also add an unsafe default, a broad permission,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.380
or a risky dependency before anyone has stopped to ask whether it belongs.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.760
Protect sensitive information first.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:28.980
Do not paste credentials, private keys, access tokens, personal data, confidential source code, internal logs,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:35.440
or customer information into a coding service unless the environment and organizational policy explicitly permit it.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.460
A useful assistant response is never worth exposing data that should stay private.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:47.960
When in doubt, replace real values with realistic examples and keep the original material inside approved systems.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.820
Generated code may introduce familiar weaknesses.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:58.000
Watch for missing authorization checks, unsafe command construction, injection risks, insecure temporary files,

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:04.460
weak secret handling, excessive permissions, overly detailed error messages, unvalidated file paths,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.520
or unsafe deserialization, which means turning data back into program objects without enough checks.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.580
These issues are not exotic.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.800
They are common ways ordinary software becomes unsafe, especially when a patch is accepted because it appears to

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.860
solve the visible happy path.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.240
Dependencies need their own review.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:29.860
An assistant may suggest a package that is unnecessary, outdated, incompatible, poorly maintained,

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.800
incorrectly licensed, or nonexistent.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:40.620
Verify the package name, official source, version, license, maintenance status, and security history before adding it.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:47.000
Installation commands and scripts also deserve inspection, because they can run downloaded code or change the machine.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.620
Use least privilege for tools and agents.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:56.480
Give access only to the repository, commands, credentials, and deployment capabilities needed for the current task.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.460
High-impact actions such as deleting data, changing infrastructure, deploying to production, using credentials,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:07.240
sending external messages, or spending money should require deliberate human approval.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.020
Convenience does not transfer accountability.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.520
The developer remains responsible for protecting systems and data.
